1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,714
Coach: A disagreement becomes easier to examine when the alternatives are clear.

2
00:00:04,714 --> 00:00:09,000
Coach: Instead of defending a preferred option against a vague problem,

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,571
Coach: ask what each proposal would involve.

4
00:00:11,571 --> 00:00:14,571
Coach: Then compare both against the same questions.

5
00:00:14,921 --> 00:00:20,921
Coach: Our fictional group is planning a twenty-minute practice session with two sample problems.

6
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:26,493
Coach: The aim is to understand the key reasoning, not simply finish more answers.

7
00:00:26,493 --> 00:00:31,207
Coach: The tool can offer a hint or a full worked answer.

8
00:00:31,207 --> 00:00:34,207
Coach: Neither kind of output has been verified.

9
00:00:34,557 --> 00:00:38,843
Maya: An answer tool can save time when we get stuck.

10
00:00:38,843 --> 00:00:43,557
Maya: I would use it as soon as a problem becomes difficult.

11
00:00:43,907 --> 00:00:48,193
Coach: Return to Maya’s original wording, before the clarification explored earlier.

12
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:51,193
Coach: It leaves the kind of help open.

13
00:00:51,193 --> 00:00:53,764
Coach: For comparison, take one possible version:

14
00:00:53,764 --> 00:00:56,336
Coach: open a full worked answer immediately.

15
00:00:56,336 --> 00:00:58,479
Coach: Set it beside another proposal:

16
00:00:58,479 --> 00:01:03,193
Coach: make a brief attempt, then request a focused hint if needed.

17
00:01:03,543 --> 00:01:05,257
Coach: Use four questions here:

18
00:01:05,257 --> 00:01:11,257
Coach: how would time be used, what reasoning would participants examine, could someone get started,

19
00:01:11,257 --> 00:01:15,114
Coach: and how would the group check the tool’s output?

20
00:01:15,114 --> 00:01:17,257
Coach: These come from this task.

21
00:01:17,257 --> 00:01:20,257
Coach: They are not a universal scoring system.

22
00:01:28,607 --> 00:01:34,179
Coach: A worked answer could provide an entry point or a sequence to examine.

23
00:01:34,179 --> 00:01:37,607
Coach: It also takes time to read and check.

24
00:01:37,607 --> 00:01:44,036
Coach: Seeing a complete solution does not show that each person understands why its steps follow.

25
00:01:44,036 --> 00:01:47,036
Coach: Those possibilities belong in the comparison together.

26
00:01:47,386 --> 00:01:53,815
Coach: A brief attempt could help someone identify a particular gap before asking for a hint.

27
00:01:53,815 --> 00:01:59,386
Coach: But an attempt without a starting point can use time without clarifying much.

28
00:01:59,386 --> 00:02:04,101
Coach: The hint may help, or it may be unclear or inaccurate.

29
00:02:04,101 --> 00:02:05,815
Coach: It still needs checking.

30
00:02:06,165 --> 00:02:10,022
You: I would prefer a brief attempt before a hint,

31
00:02:10,022 --> 00:02:13,880
You: because it might show us which step needs help.

32
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:18,594
You: How would we support someone who cannot find a first step?

33
00:02:18,944 --> 00:02:22,801
Coach: This reply explains a preference and names a limit.

34
00:02:22,801 --> 00:02:26,658
Coach: It does not prove that the proposal is faster.

35
00:02:26,658 --> 00:02:30,944
Coach: Check whether your comparison gives both options the same chance:

36
00:02:30,944 --> 00:02:36,515
Coach: do not count checking time for one while pretending the other needs none.

37
00:02:36,865 --> 00:02:41,579
Coach: Checking could include testing a suggested step against the problem’s conditions

38
00:02:41,579 --> 00:02:43,722
Coach: and asking why it follows.

39
00:02:43,722 --> 00:02:48,008
Coach: If the group cannot verify it, keep that uncertainty visible.

40
00:02:48,008 --> 00:02:52,722
Coach: A fluent explanation from the tool is not, by itself, confirmation.

41
00:02:53,072 --> 00:02:56,501
Coach: Choose one criterion and compare both proposals privately.

42
00:02:56,501 --> 00:02:59,929
Coach: Include a possible benefit or limit for each.

43
00:02:59,929 --> 00:03:02,501
Coach: You can pause for more time.

44
00:03:02,501 --> 00:03:05,072
Coach: The aim is a fair comparison,

45
00:03:05,072 --> 00:03:09,358
Coach: not finding the sentence that makes your preferred option win.

46
00:03:21,708 --> 00:03:24,708
You: A full answer might help us start,

47
00:03:24,708 --> 00:03:28,565
You: but we would still need to check its reasoning.

48
00:03:28,565 --> 00:03:31,136
You: An attempt might reveal a gap,

49
00:03:31,136 --> 00:03:34,565
You: but someone could need help before making progress.

50
00:03:34,915 --> 00:03:37,058
Coach: No winner has been proved.

51
00:03:37,058 --> 00:03:40,487
Coach: The comparison identifies something the proposal must handle:

52
00:03:40,487 --> 00:03:43,487
Coach: getting started without losing attention to reasoning.

53
00:03:43,487 --> 00:03:47,344
Coach: Next, we will revise a suggestion around that concern

54
00:03:47,344 --> 00:03:50,344
Coach: while keeping agreement and open questions distinct.
